{=============================================================================
  Turbocompressor optimal-regime similarity laws
  CoolSolve Library model CSL-0177
 -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
  Teaching exercise (ULiege, Machines et systemes thermiques, 2004-10-12,
  exercise 5). A turbocompressor is designed to compress 2000 m3/h of air
  from 1 bar, 20 C to 3 bar at 10000 rpm. The file evaluates the two
  constants of the optimal-regime laws at that design point. The question of
  the new speed and flow at 0.9 bar, 50 C and 2.5 bar is stated in the
  original and solved in a separate continuation file.

  Inputs : V_dot_su, p_su, t_su, p_ex, rpm (design point)
  Outputs: M_r, N_r, cste_1, cste_2
  Units  : SI - temperatures in °C, pressures in Pa, energies in J
  Source : ULiege MSTh course - see README.md
=============================================================================}

"Design point of the turbocompressor, as in the original"
V_dot_su=2000/3600         "suction volume flow rate [m3/s]"
p_su=1E5                   "suction pressure [Pa]"
t_su=20                    "suction temperature [C]"
p_ex=3E5                   "discharge pressure [Pa]"
rpm=10000                  "rotation speed [rev/min]"
"Reduced variables, as in the original"
M_r=M_dot*sqrt(t_su+273)/(p_su/1E5)  "reduced mass flow; temperature in K and pressure in bar, as in the original [kg-K^0.5/(s-bar)]"
M_dot=V_dot_su/v_su        "mass flow rate [kg/s]"
v_su=VOLUME(Air,T=T_su,P=P_su)  "suction specific volume [m3/kg]"
N_r=N/sqrt(t_su+273)       "reduced rotation speed [1/(s-K^0.5)]"
N=rpm/60                   "rotation speed [rev/s]"
cste_1=r_p/M_r^2           "constant of the optimal-regime laws, as in the original [s^2-bar^2/(kg^2-K)]"
r_p=p_ex/p_su              "pressure ratio [-]"
cste_2=N_r/M_r             "constant of the optimal-regime laws, as in the original [bar/(kg-K)]"
